An if statement allows your code to make decisions. It checks a condition, which is a comparison that results in either true or false. If the condition is true, the code inside the if block runs. If the condition is false, the code inside the else block runs instead.

Use the === operator to check if two values are strictly equal. This is different from the = operator, which is used for assignment.

Here is an example:

const score = 50;
if (score === 100) {
  console.log("Perfect");
} else {
  console.log("Keep trying");
}

In this sample, the code compares the value of score to 100. Since 50 is not equal to 100, the condition is false. Therefore, the program skips the first block and executes the else block, printing "Keep trying" to the console.

