A function uses parameters to receive inputs and return to send a result back to the caller.

function calculateTotal(price) {
  return price + 2;
}

const finalAmount = calculateTotal(20);

In this example, calculateTotal is the function name and price is the parameter. When we call calculateTotal(20), the value 20 is the argument. The function adds 2 to the argument and returns 22. The variable finalAmount then stores that returned value.

If you used console.log(price + 2) instead of return, the function would display the result in the console, but the caller would receive undefined because the function did not explicitly return a value.

